    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d272">

                <pb facs="0426" n="408" xml:id="pg408"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.00/1–2746: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-27T14:00:00+01:00">January 27, 1946—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:08 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>434. Léon Blum and Vincent Auriol wanted to know last night what I thought of
                    Gouin’s letter (mytel No. 430, January
                        26<anchor n="12" xml:id="d272-f1"/>). I replied, “The letter is excellent,
                    but will you be able to change the words into acts”. They both replied, “At
                    least we will make a very serious effort to do so”. I said, “What about the
                    Communist[s] and the MRP”. They replied, “In the
                    end they both said they would go along with us”.</p>
                <p>It was obvious that Blum and Auriol both felt that the Socialists have come out
                    of all this very well: Gouin in the Presidency, Socialist Ministers in various
                    key posts and Auriol probably to be elected President of the Assembly. Also
                    there was an intimation that they were glad De Gaulle is out of the government.
                    However, this is coupled with the knowledge that they have taken on a very heavy
                    responsibility and that if they fail it is “curtains” for them.</p>
                <p>Repeated to London as 85.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d272-f1" n="12" xml:id="d272-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed (851.00/1–2646); it summarized the letter of January 25 by M. Gouin
                    referred to in the final paragraph of telegram 432 from Paris, January 27, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
